--TEST--
A sibling's throw cancels the rest of the group, and a cancelled task's `catch` and the statements after it never run: the throw propagates as itself with nothing of the group still running — `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns` and `rule:concurrency/cancellation-runs-no-user-code`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/a-child-s-throw
// § 4's "one child throws" row, and § 5's rule about what a cancelled child may
// run on its way out — which is nothing. The deadline path is pinned by
// tests/conformance/core/a-cancelled-task-dies-where-it-sleeps.nvst; this is
// the other trigger, and the one where the propagating value is a `Throwable`
// the caller wrote rather than a `TimeoutError` the runtime made.
class Trace {
    public static int $caught = 0;
    public static int $ranOn = 0;
}

try {
    Core\Task::all({
        // Parked long enough that the thrower is certainly first, so the
        // "first by completion order" tie-break is not what this case rests on.
        sleeper: fn(): int => {
            try {
                Core\Time::sleep(2s);
            } catch (Throwable $e) {
                // Cancellation is not a `Throwable` (§ 5, and `rule:errors/throwable-hierarchy` for
                // the same reason a limit report is not one), so this arm is
                // unreachable — a runtime that cancelled by throwing would
                // count here.
                Trace::$caught += 1;
            }
            // Nor does the wait merely end early. A child resumed and left to
            // continue would count here instead.
            Trace::$ranOn += 1;
            return 1;
        },
        thrower: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(10ms);
            throw new RuntimeError("the first throw");
        },
    });
    echo "the group returned", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    // "the **first** throw propagates" — as itself, not wrapped in a group
    // error and not converted to the `TimeoutError` the deadline row uses.
    echo "propagated ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// Read immediately after the `catch`, which is only a meaningful reading
// because § 4 already waited for the cancellations: at this line there is no
// sibling left that could still move either counter.
echo Trace::$caught, " caught, ", Trace::$ranOn, " ran on", "\n";
--EXPECT--
propagated the first throw
0 caught, 0 ran on
